<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">1936</h1><p class="ref-lede">1936 in world history: 47 events, 15 births, 29 deaths, 6 works, 8 states founded and 5 that came to an end. 720 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/1932">1932</a><a href="/years/1933">1933</a><a href="/years/1934">1934</a><a href="/years/1935">1935</a><a href="/years/1936" aria-current="page">1936</a><a href="/years/1937">1937</a><a href="/years/1938">1938</a><a href="/years/1939">1939</a><a href="/years/1940">1940</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-20">20 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">George V dies and Edward VIII becomes king</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> died at Sandringham, and his eldest son succeeded as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-viii">Edward VIII</a>. Edward abdicated before the end of the year to marry Wallis Simpson, the only voluntary abdication in British history.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/versailles" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/versailles.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-7">7 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">German troops reoccupy the Rhineland</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Hitler sent soldiers into the demilitarised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> in breach of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locarno">Locarno</a> treaties. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> did not respond with force, encouraging further German expansion.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-18">18 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Military uprising launches the Spanish Civil War</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Following the army revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> the day before, garrisons across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> rose against the Republic. The war lasted nearly three years and ended in General Franco's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-3">3 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Jesse Owens wins the 100 metres at the Berlin Olympics</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Jesse Owens won the 100 metres, the first of four gold medals at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> Games. The success of a Black American athlete undercut Nazi propaganda about racial superiority.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-world-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-2">2 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The BBC launches the world's first regular high-definition TV service</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">From Alexandra Palace in north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, the BBC began a regular public television service in what was then called high definition. It was suspended during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and resumed in 1946.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-11">11 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Edward VIII abdicates to marry Wallis Simpson</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The abdication took legal effect, and that evening Edward broadcast that he could not reign 'without the help and support of the woman I love'. His brother succeeded as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-vi">George VI</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/anti-comintern-pact" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anti-comintern-pact.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/anti-comintern-pact">Anti-Comintern Pact</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Anti-Comintern Pact was an anti-communist agreement concluded between Nazi Germany and the Empire of Japan on 25 November 1936, directed against the Communist International.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/four-year-plan" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/four-year-plan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/four-year-plan">Four Year Plan</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Four Year Plan was an economic programme launched by Nazi Germany in 1936 under Hermann Göring to make the country ready for war within four years.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/xian-incident" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xian-incident.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Crisis</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/xian-incident">Xi'an Incident</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Xi'an Incident was the detention of Chiang Kai-shek in Xi'an in December 1936 by his own generals Zhang Xueliang and Yang Hucheng, who wanted him to stop fighting the Communists and resist Japan.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/franco-syrian-treaty-of-independence" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franco-syrian-treaty-of-independence.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/franco-syrian-treaty-of-independence">Franco-Syrian Treaty of Independence</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Franco-Syrian Treaty of Independence was negotiated in 1936 between France and the Syrian nationalists of the National Bloc after a long general strike.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/anglo-egyptian-treaty-of-1936">Anglo-Egyptian Treaty of 1936</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Anglo-Egyptian Treaty of 1936 was an agreement between the United Kingdom and the Kingdom of Egypt.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/hull-alfaro-treaty">Hull–Alfaro Treaty</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Hull–Alfaro Treaty was signed in 1936 by US Secretary of State Cordell Hull and the Panamanian envoy Ricardo J. Alfaro, and ratified by the United States in 1939.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/jarrow-march">Jarrow March</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Jarrow March, or Jarrow Crusade, was a protest march in October 1936 by about 200 unemployed men from the shipbuilding town of Jarrow in north-east England to London, to petition Parliament for work after the closure of Palmer's shipyard.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/1936-summer-olympics">1936 Summer Olympics</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The 1936 Summer Olympics were held in Berlin in August 1936.</p></div></li></ol></section>
